#Style

One visual vocabulary for every video. A colour means the same thing
wherever it appears: the yellow of a module title in a source listing is
the yellow of a module node in the name-graph, and the green of a claim
body is the green of a test node. The viewer learns the palette once, in
the source, and can then read the graph without a legend.

The same split runs through the typography. Prose is set in a serif,
code in a monospace -- literate programming's two voices, visibly
different on the page.

##Frame

Square, for the timeline the videos are headed to: 1080 pixels a side,
and an equally square frame of eight manim units so layouts reason in
units without caring about pixels.

    FRAME_PIXELS = 1080
    FRAME_UNITS = 8.0
    MARGIN = 0.4
    BACKGROUND = "#0E1116"

    def _square(scene_class) -> None:
        config.output_file = scene_class.__name__
        config.pixel_width = FRAME_PIXELS
        config.pixel_height = FRAME_PIXELS
        config.frame_width = FRAME_UNITS
        config.frame_height = FRAME_UNITS
        config.background_color = BACKGROUND
        config.media_dir = "../media"

A movie for animated scenes, a single PNG for stills. Stills are how a
layout gets checked quickly, without scrubbing through a video. Each
output is named for its scene: manim's config is global, and would
otherwise carry one render's file name into the next.

    def render_square(scene_class) -> None:
        _square(scene_class)
        config.write_to_movie = True
        config.save_last_frame = False
        scene_class().render()

    def render_still(scene_class) -> None:
        _square(scene_class)
        config.write_to_movie = False
        config.save_last_frame = True
        scene_class().render()

Anything placed on screen can be fitted inside the margins: centred, and
shrunk if it is too big. It is only ever scaled down, never up, so small
things stay their natural size.

    def fit_to_frame(mobject):
        room = FRAME_UNITS - 2 * MARGIN
        factor = min(room / mobject.width, room / mobject.height, 1.0)
        return mobject.scale(factor).move_to([0, 0, 0])

~example
    fit_to_frame(Square(side_length=20)).width <= FRAME_UNITS - 2 * MARGIN
    fit_to_frame(Square(side_length=1)).width == 1
    fit_to_frame(Square(side_length=1).shift([5, -3, 0])).get_center().tolist() == [0, 0, 0]

##Captions

There is no voice-over; a beat's words sit in a band along the bottom
of the frame, set in the prose face, so the picture above never runs
under them. A picture that shares the frame with a caption is fitted to
the space above the band instead of the whole frame.

    CAPTION_BAND = 1.0
    CAPTION_HEIGHT = 0.32

    def fit_above_caption(mobject):
        room = FRAME_UNITS - 2 * MARGIN
        factor = min(room / mobject.width, (room - CAPTION_BAND) / mobject.height, 1.0)
        return mobject.scale(factor).move_to([0, CAPTION_BAND / 2, 0])

Smaller text elsewhere on screen is sized the same way as labels in the
game board: by the height of a capital, so words with and without
descenders come out at one size.

    def sized_text(words: str, font: str, colour: str, cap: float):
        text = Text(words, font=font, color=colour, font_size=72)
        return text.scale(cap / Text("H", font=font, font_size=72).height)

    def caption(words: str):
        text = Text(words, font=PROSE_FONT, color=PROSE, font_size=72)
        text.scale_to_fit_height(CAPTION_HEIGHT)
        return text.move_to([0, -FRAME_UNITS / 2 + MARGIN + CAPTION_BAND / 2, 0])

A fitted picture and its caption never overlap.

~example
    fit_above_caption(Square(side_length=20)).get_bottom()[1] >= caption("Every claim, checked").get_top()[1]

##Source Colours

Kinds of source text, as the highlighter classifies them, with a colour
and a font family each.

    PROSE_FONT = "Georgia"
    CODE_FONT = "Consolas"

    TITLE = "#F4D35E"
    SUBHEADING = "#EE964B"
    PROSE = "#E8E6E3"
    REF = "#4CC9F0"
    CODE = "#8EC5E8"
    SIGIL = "#C77DFF"
    CLAIM = "#7BC67E"
    STRUCTURE = "#6C7380"

Inside code, the language's own syntax is coloured too, but quietly: a
cool, muted sub-palette that never borrows a notlob colour. Identifiers
keep the code blue -- the same blue as symbol nodes in the graph --
keywords are nearly white, literals a desaturated tan, comments recede.
Structure stays loud and syntax stays soft, so the notlob colours still
read at a glance.

    KEYWORD = "#E3ECF5"
    LITERAL = "#C8B79A"
    COMMENT = "#5E6875"
    PUNCT = "#7F8C99"

Claims are code as well, and are coloured as code. What marks them as
claims is a bar of claim green in the margin beside them, set one
character width clear of the text.

    CLAIM_BAR_WIDTH = 3
    CLAIM_BAR_GAP = 1.0

    TOKEN_STYLES = {
        "title": (TITLE, PROSE_FONT),
        "subheading": (SUBHEADING, PROSE_FONT),
        "prose": (PROSE, PROSE_FONT),
        "ref": (REF, PROSE_FONT),
        "bullet": (PROSE, PROSE_FONT),
        "code": (CODE, CODE_FONT),
        "keyword": (KEYWORD, CODE_FONT),
        "literal": (LITERAL, CODE_FONT),
        "comment": (COMMENT, CODE_FONT),
        "punct": (PUNCT, CODE_FONT),
        "sigil": (SIGIL, CODE_FONT),
        "claim": (CLAIM, CODE_FONT),
        "structure": (STRUCTURE, CODE_FONT),
    }

##Graph Colours

Node colours reuse the source colours of the text each node comes from.
Every kind of claim -- example, property, test -- is claim green; a run
block keeps its sigil's violet. Edges are quiet except REFERENCES: a
prose cross-reference is the literate link, and it keeps the cyan it has
in the source.

    NODE_COLOURS = {
        "MODULE": TITLE,
        "SUBHEADING": SUBHEADING,
        "SYMBOL": CODE,
        "EXAMPLE": CLAIM,
        "PROPERTY": CLAIM,
        "TEST": CLAIM,
        "RUN": SIGIL,
        "EXTERNAL": STRUCTURE,
    }

    EDGE_COLOURS = {
        "CONTAINS": STRUCTURE,
        "DEFINES": STRUCTURE,
        "IMPORTS": TITLE,
        "USES": CODE,
        "USES_EXTERNAL": STRUCTURE,
        "REFERENCES": REF,
    }

Edges differ in weight as well as colour. The tree edges inside a
module are quiet; uses between symbols are the faintest of all, so a
hundred of them read as texture rather than tangle; imports and
cross-references keep their colour but stay light enough that a module
which references everything -- an overview, say -- does not swamp the
picture. Any single edge the story is about is brought up separately,
at full strength.

    EDGE_OPACITY = {
        "CONTAINS": 0.35,
        "DEFINES": 0.35,
        "IMPORTS": 0.25,
        "USES": 0.12,
        "USES_EXTERNAL": 0.35,
        "REFERENCES": 0.4,
    }

    EDGE_WIDTH = {
        "CONTAINS": 1.5,
        "DEFINES": 1.5,
        "IMPORTS": 1.5,
        "USES": 1.0,
        "USES_EXTERNAL": 1.5,
        "REFERENCES": 1.5,
    }

The palette has to cover whatever notlob can produce. The graph schema
is notlob's own statement of that, so the check reads it directly.

~example
    schema_kinds("Node") <= set(NODE_COLOURS)
    schema_kinds("Edge") <= set(EDGE_COLOURS)
    schema_kinds("Edge") <= set(EDGE_OPACITY) & set(EDGE_WIDTH)

---

#Appendix Schema

The name-graph JSON schema, from the notlob reference clone.

    NAME_GRAPH_SCHEMA = Path("../ref-projects/notlob/notlob/schema/name_graph.json")

    def schema_kinds(definition: str) -> set:
        schema = json.loads(NAME_GRAPH_SCHEMA.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        return set(schema["definitions"][definition]["properties"]["kind"]["enum"])

#References
    import json
    from pathlib import Path

    from manim import Square, Text, config
